    answerId: "b",
    explanation: "The slanted line cuts the left square into two triangles, the two halves are squares, and the whole drawing is a rectangle that is not a square. No group of lines closes up into a shape with exactly 5 sides and 5 corners. A rectangle that is not a square is tempting if you only look at the small pieces, but the whole outline is one.",
    hints: ["Look at small pieces and at bigger shapes made of several pieces of Ishita's drawing. Check the whole outline too."],
    figure: {"type": "svg", "markup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 224 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A rectangle split into two squares, with one slanted line in the left square\">\n  <rect class=\"bg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"224\" height=\"170\" fill=\"#fff\"/>\n  <text class=\"title\" x=\"112.0\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333\">Ishita's drawing</text>\n  <line class=\"part seg\" x1=\"24\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#263238\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n  <line class=\"part seg\" x1=\"24\" y1=\"128\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#263238\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n  <line class=\"part seg\" x1=\"24\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#263238\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n  <line class=\"part seg\" x1=\"112\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#263238\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n  <line class=\"part seg\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#263238\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n  <line class=\"part seg\" x1=\"24\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#263238\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n</svg>", "alt": "A rectangle twice as wide as it is tall, split by an upright line into two equal squares. In the left square, a slanted line goes from the top left corner to the bottom right corner of that square."}
